  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1213 PM MST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nA pattern change will arrive on Wednesday as a trough swings into \nthe Rockies. A cold front will move across the area, late Tuesday \nnight or early Wednesday morning, leading to high temperatures in \nthe 40s and 50s--roughly average for this time of year. This front \nwill be mostly dry, with predominately west winds promoting a dry \ndownslope environment. The mountains, however, could see a quick \nshot of snow, with the Sierra Madre Range picking up a few inches of \naccumulation. The Snowys will likely be shadowed and not see much in \nthe way of accumulations. The bigger concern with the frontal \npassage will be strong winds. More recent model runs show a strong \npotential for high winds. MSLP gradients will compress from the \nfront as well as CAG to CPR 850 and 700 mb heights. Height values \nare rather impressive, with 850 mb heights reaching over 70 meters \nand 700 mb heights well over 60 meters. These values are typically \nindicative of strong high winds around the Arlington area. Winds \naloft also look much stronger compared to previous runs of the GFS. \n750 mb winds max out at over 70 kts over the Laramie Range by mid-\nday with very strong subsidence. Subsidence alone will likely be \nenough to allow these winds to reach the surface at Bordeaux and the \nSouth Laramie Range and foothills. Could even see high winds in the \nwind prone adjacent areas along Interstate 25 as cross sections\nof omega and potential temperature show a strong mountain wave \nsignature. Winds gusts between 60 and 70 MPH will be likely, \nespecially in the wind prones, with stronger gusts possible if \nthis trend holds. Winds will weaken by Wednesday evening as the \ntrough moves out of the CWA.\n\nSeasonal temperatures will stick around on Thursday with little to \nno weather impacts expected. Some lingering breezy conditions are \nstill possible on Thursday, but overall, will be a quiet day. Friday \ninto the weekend, things could get interesting with a potential \nwinter storm. Global models continue to trend QPF/snowfall totals \ndown across the forecast area as the trough becomes more of a \nprogressive open wave instead of a closed off low. Many ensemble \nmembers of both the GFS and the ECMWF don't even show any QPF for \nareas east of the Laramie Range, a far cry from the runs of a few \ndays ago showing a major winter storm. Of course, its still over a \nweek away, so models could change, but what is more certain is that \nit will likely be cooler and windier during this time period.",
